WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:13.280
Hello everyone, I'm Dr. Emilia Javorsky from the&nbsp;
Future of Life Institute where at the institute I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:19.280
lead up our efforts on lethal autonomous weapons.&nbsp;
And I am super thrilled to be here today to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:25.840
speak with this amazing group of people who hail&nbsp;
from very different backgrounds and perspectives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:32.000
but something they all should have in common is a&nbsp;
great dedication and work on working to safeguard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.760
our world from the from lethal autonomous weapons.&nbsp;
And so we're coming together today because we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:45.280
really at a pivotal moment in this issue for the&nbsp;
world to decide what our future will look like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:52.240
with autonomous weapons which we define as&nbsp;
weapons that use AI to select and apply force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:59.280
against targets like human beings without human&nbsp;
intervention. And over the next few weeks the UN&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:05.920
is slated to meet in Geneva at something called&nbsp;
the Convention on Certain Conventional Weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:11.440
to debate this issue to debate what should&nbsp;
the world do about the threat of autonomous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:18.720
weapons. And at the heart of that question is the&nbsp;
determination that this is the moment where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:25.600
global community is going to make the decision,&nbsp;
"Are we going to allow AI to decide who lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:32.480
and who dies?" And because of this urgency&nbsp;
and because of the magnitude of the issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:38.960
that we're dealing with at the Future of Life&nbsp;
Institute, we are releasing a short film that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:45.120
is really a follow-up to a film we released back&nbsp;
in 2017 called Slaughterbots. When we released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:51.520
that first film, that was really a film aimed&nbsp;
to raise awareness about the issue of lethal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:56.800
autonomous weapons and sort of put it on the map.&nbsp;
Now, three short years later sort of since we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:03.280
released that film, we're living in a world in&nbsp;
which we're already seeing media reports across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:09.280
the globe of small companies producing these types&nbsp;
of weapons and them being used on the battlefield.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:15.680
And so we decided to create another short film to&nbsp;
both follow up in warning people of that risk but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:20.960
also depicting what is the kind of thinking that's&nbsp;
happening right now that's driving us towards that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.440
end point. And, more importantly, what is it&nbsp;
that we can do about it and what can the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:31.600
do about it given this really important moment&nbsp;
that we have with this UN meeting to perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:39.120
steer ourselves in another direction. So that was&nbsp;
why we put that film together. But the panel today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.840
is to kind of from the realm of storytelling into&nbsp;
the realm of reality and hearing these different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:50.000
perspectives on the state of lethal autonomous&nbsp;
weapons and also what are our policy options&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:56.240
for moving forward. And so with that I would&nbsp;
like to bring together onstage our great panel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:02.960
and I would like each person to sort of kick this&nbsp;
off by introducing themselves and telling us a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:08.800
little bit about who you are, what organization&nbsp;
you're with, and your perspective on this issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:15.360
So I will start off with Max. It's an&nbsp;
honor to be here. I'm Max Tegmark, yeah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:22.640
I'm a Professor at MIT where I work on a&nbsp;
little bit of physics. But mostly AI research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:27.360
for the past five years. And I'm also one of&nbsp;
the founders of the Future of Life Institute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.320
because I love technology and I believe&nbsp;
that artificial intelligence in particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:38.240
has the potential to be the most awesome&nbsp;
technology ever helping life flourish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:44.560
like never before. But it also has the potential&nbsp;
to be the worst thing ever to happen to humanity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:49.280
And I think this issue of lethal autonomous&nbsp;
weapons is really really the first fork in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:55.280
road where we have to make the right decision.&nbsp;
Because the key to a wonderful future where ai&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:01.840
helps us cure diseases and helps live&nbsp;
let's live healthy and wealthy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.680
inspiring lives or is to draw a clear red&nbsp;
line between all of these wonderful uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:15.840
and the small set of really really terrible&nbsp;
uses which would ruin everything. And if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:23.520
can't even agree that we don't want to use&nbsp;
ai to anonymously kill people and to decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.240
entirely on its own who lives and who dies now&nbsp;
who are we kidding that we're ever going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:34.080
able to draw any kind of lines just be our ai&nbsp;
towards towards the good future that we all want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:42.800
Awesome. Thank you Max. Stuart. So i'm Stuart&nbsp;
Russell, I'm a Professor of computer science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:48.880
at the University of California Berkeley. I've&nbsp;
been doing artificial intelligence there for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:58.720
more than 35 years. And I'm also director of&nbsp;
the Center for Human Compatible Artificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:06.960
Intelligence and like Max, I am very&nbsp;
concerned that AI be beneficial for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:12.640
the human race in future. And I share his&nbsp;
optimism that it can be enormously beneficial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:22.400
I've been concerned about autonomous weapons since&nbsp;
