00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:07.280
machines the power to kill people is just is just&nbsp;
crossing a line that that's unacceptable to us. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.400
we're working as a partnership to&nbsp;
secure a legal outcome on this issue

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:18.800
Maya. So, my name is Maya Briem. I'm&nbsp;
a legal advisor at the International&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:26.640
Committee of the Red Cross or in short ICRC.&nbsp;
The ICRC's exclusively humanitarian mission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:32.080
is to protect the lives and the dignity of&nbsp;
persons affected by armed conflict, war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:38.960
and other situations of violence. And the&nbsp;
ICRC acts to preserve people's lives and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:46.320
livelihoods and provides assistance to them,&nbsp;
to people in need. And we also seek to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:53.280
suffering by promoting and strengthening&nbsp;
international humanitarian law the IHL.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:59.600
That's the law applicable to war and broader&nbsp;
universal humanitarian principles. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:09.120
part of the ICRC's work since its inception is to&nbsp;
support states in the development of international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:16.880
humanitarian law including to prohibit or regulate&nbsp;
certain weapons based on a realistic assessment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:24.560
of their adverse humanitarian impacts. So in the&nbsp;
past the ICRC's, the ICRC has played an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:31.760
role for example in the processes that led to&nbsp;
treaties prohibiting anti-personnel landmines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:38.000
or the treaty prohibiting cluster munitions.&nbsp;
And in more recent years the ICRC has given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:46.480
focused attention to emerging technologies of&nbsp;
warfare. So, new forms of waging war, new weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:54.320
enabled by developments in science and technology,&nbsp;
including of course autonomous weapon systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:59.920
Wonderful. Thank you all so much for being here&nbsp;
today. I can already tell in sort of our opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:05.600
statements, there's a lot of differences&nbsp;
in sort of our perspectives of concern. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:10.480
I already sense that everyone has some degree&nbsp;
of optimism that something can be done on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:17.120
which is is a good place to start. So I'm really&nbsp;
excited to sort of dig into the content here on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:23.440
autonomous weapon systems you know. From from my&nbsp;
perspective, I'm a physician by training, and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:30.560
came to this issue in thinking about how we think&nbsp;
through the use of ai and life and death decisions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:36.640
in a very different context - which is in the&nbsp;
context of saving lives. And in that thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:42.240
our professional association the American Medical&nbsp;
Association developed a policy in which they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:48.080
even recognize artificial intelligence. They say&nbsp;
when it comes to life and death decisions it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:53.760
called augmented intelligence because there's&nbsp;
an irreplaceable role of a human being and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:10:00.080
human judgment when it comes to thinking through&nbsp;
decisions that affect a human being's health and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:05.760
well-being. And so it always struck me that it was&nbsp;
totally absurd that on this parallel conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:12.400
we're having about hey let's use AI to kill people&nbsp;
without humans in the loop right? This strikes for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:19.120
me a really deep moral problem right? And that&nbsp;
there is a fundamental moral issue here with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:25.200
allowing algorithms to make the decision over who&nbsp;
lives and who dies. And so the moral dimension is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:30.400
certainly I think one. But I'd be interested to&nbsp;
know from each of your different perspectives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:34.960
how do you think through what are the problems&nbsp;
with lethal autonomous weapon systems? Why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:39.680
are they so bad? And why are we all here sort of&nbsp;
dedicating our time to do something about them?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:44.480
So I'll kick that off with Stuart because I know&nbsp;
you've been thinking through different dimensions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:51.040
of this for quite some time. Thanks Emilia. Yeah&nbsp;
I think the moral dimension is very important. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:57.040
I know that some very significant figures&nbsp;
including the Secretary General of the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:05.360
Nations believe that the moral aspect is in fact&nbsp;
decisive that these are morally unacceptable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:13.840
weapons. Even if I share that view and I think I&nbsp;
do. I don't think it's going to be persuasive to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:21.200
governments who don't trust the moral behavior&nbsp;
of other governments. Because even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:25.680
the moral governments might refrain from using&nbsp;
such weapons, they're not going to trust the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:32.160
other governments to do the same. And so they're&nbsp;
