00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:40.000
When I think about autonomous weapons, to me&nbsp;
the main characteristic that they have that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:44.640
different from other weapons, we have some&nbsp;
pretty horrible weapons that are so-called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:49.760
allowed already, the main difference is&nbsp;
that they don't require a human being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:55.840
to be there supervising or pulling&nbsp;
the trigger or launching the missile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:03.120
And if you know anything about computer science&nbsp;
what we know is if a computer can do something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:08.880
once, it can do it a million times or a billion&nbsp;
times. They're very very good at doing the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:14.800
thing over and over again in vast quantities. We&nbsp;
call this scalability in computer science and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:21.040
a it's seen as an asset for computers because&nbsp;
it it lets them do lots of tedious calculations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:26.880
without a human having to insert themselves&nbsp;
in every single one of those calculations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:32.880
But when it comes to killing, that means that&nbsp;
you can launch a million or 10 million weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:40.480
simultaneously you know from a small control&nbsp;
center and wipe out entire human populations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:47.120
You can do it selectively so you could wipe out&nbsp;
you know all males of military age for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:54.640
you could wipe out all persons visibly belonging&nbsp;
to a religious group or an ethnic group.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.760 --> 00:13:04.320
And do we want to be part of this kind of&nbsp;
weapon? It would seem to me that they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:10.960
replace nuclear weapons in almost all of the&nbsp;
roles that people have for nuclear weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:18.960
because they're cheaper, they're easier to build,&nbsp;
they don't leave behind a radioactive crater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:26.640
they leave all the physical assets intact, and&nbsp;
we don't sell nuclear weapons in supermarkets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:34.480
for a good reason. It's not a moral reason. It's&nbsp;
a practical reason. And if we allow these kinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:39.040
of weapons to go ahead it will be just as if we&nbsp;
were selling nuclear weapons in the supermarket.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:47.040
So, democratized weapons of mass destruction sound&nbsp;
deeply concerning for to me at least. Max is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:51.840
other sort of technologist on the panel. What&nbsp;
what are your reactions to what Stewart said.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:56.080
Yeah i'd like to follow up on the supermarket&nbsp;
comment that Stuart made there because I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.720 --> 00:14:00.480
the biggest misconception that&nbsp;
we see in a lot of governments&nbsp;&nbsp;

