00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:28.960
Yeah, I mean I think this this is an issue&nbsp;
where you can bring together and justifiably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:34.000
there are a wide range of different problem&nbsp;
framings as I would describe them. And i think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:41.200
in my mind I tend to work out with from the sort&nbsp;
of narrower to the to the wider. I feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:48.320
at a more, at the most fundamental level where&nbsp;
I see a sort of moral set of moral anxieties is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:53.520
in any of these machine systems you know you're&nbsp;
using sensors and you know computers to analyze&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.520 --> 00:17:00.080
sensor data inevitably you know machines don't&nbsp;
see people. They don't see humans as humans. They,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:05.840
they're categorizing the world into certain&nbsp;
pre-programmed classification structures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.840 --> 00:17:11.440
they're applying labels to things based within&nbsp;
certain rigid structures. And if we're applying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:16.320
labels to things through machines and then&nbsp;
labeling them to be subject to the application&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:21.840
of force there's a reduction in that process&nbsp;
of the reality of human existence into simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:27.680
a pattern of data to be to be processed in a,&nbsp;
in a mechanical way effectively. So, in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:33.440
process of functioning I think when it comes to&nbsp;
machines targeting people say there is immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:39.200
a degree of sort of reduction and a loss of&nbsp;
our humanity in that in that sort of process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:45.520
I think that's there fundamentally but I think it&nbsp;
just gets worse if you know in Stewart's examples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:51.120
if you start to imagine how either deliberately&nbsp;
or inadvertently because of the biases inherent in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:58.160
certain data sets you start to identify as enemies&nbsp;
people of particular skin colors or people of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:04.560
particular genders and the like. I mean the way&nbsp;
we start to formalize these these processes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:08.800
prejudice in the world and and tie them into the&nbsp;
application of force I think there's, there's all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:14.080
sorts of concerns in that space. So again, drawing&nbsp;
a line against systems that would target people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.080 --> 00:18:19.920
I think is a is a fundamental moral moral response&nbsp;
