00:36:34.720 --> 00:36:41.200
Maya on from your perspective I think the word&nbsp;
urgent does appear in the ICRC's recommendations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:41.200 --> 00:36:48.560
to the rep cons how how do you guys think through&nbsp;
this topic? Sure we've, we've in fact called since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:48.560 --> 00:36:56.640
2015 on states to agree international limits&nbsp;
on autonomous weapons. So that the urgency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:56.640 --> 00:37:01.920
is certainly here. I'd also like to sort of&nbsp;
reflect a little bit on what others have said.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:02.960 --> 00:37:09.920
It's important I think to keep in mind that&nbsp;
developments in this area developments on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:09.920 --> 00:37:14.480
kinds of technologies that are developed the kinds&nbsp;
of weapon technologies that are developed are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:14.480 --> 00:37:21.680
result of of choices they are not pre-determined.&nbsp;
So resistance is not futile and I would agree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:27.040
with Richard that we do have an opportunity&nbsp;
here. And we are actually in a fairly sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:29.280 --> 00:37:37.840
a good place in the sense that we have a deep and&nbsp;
wide-ranging debate on on this issue already. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:38.880 --> 00:37:43.840
I think we should should keep that sort of this&nbsp;
this this this sense of of urgency but also the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:43.840 --> 00:37:49.840
sense of opportunity at the forefront of our&nbsp;
minds when we're discussing autonomous weapons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:54.480 --> 00:38:00.560
For us what what is remarkable is that in pres&nbsp;
so as I said we understand autonomous weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:00.560 --> 00:38:07.520
in a way that includes certain existing weapon&nbsp;
systems. And in present-day military practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:07.520 --> 00:38:14.960
the the use of these these autonomous weapons is&nbsp;
tends to be strictly limited in ways that can help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:14.960 --> 00:38:22.400
reduce humanitarian risks. And that can facilitate&nbsp;
compliance with international humanitarian law.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:22.400 --> 00:38:32.720
So for example an autonomous air defense system&nbsp;
these tend to be used only to target typical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:32.720 --> 00:38:41.280
military objects in this case incoming missiles&nbsp;
or military aircraft. And in in this case but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:41.280 --> 00:38:50.160
also other existing autonomous weapons I think&nbsp;
it is significant that the allegations of use of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:50.160 --> 00:38:58.880
autonomous weapons to directly target people they&nbsp;
are emphatically refuted by alleged users. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:58.880 --> 00:39:04.160
this is significant because it speaks to one of&nbsp;
those moral lines that others have evoked as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:04.160 --> 00:39:10.720
is that in present military practice autonomous&nbsp;
weapons are not used to target persons directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.720 --> 00:39:16.720
with the exception of anti-personnel landmines&nbsp;
which have been outlawed by treaty to which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:16.720 --> 00:39:24.080
a majority of states has signed up to. Now in&nbsp;
existing practice autonomous weapons also tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:24.080 --> 00:39:31.520
be used only for limited time time frames limited&nbsp;
duration in well-defined geographical zones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:40.240
and in situations where civilians and civilian&nbsp;
objects such as civilian aircraft for instance are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:40.240 --> 00:39:46.400
are not present so could be around a military&nbsp;
base where civilian aircraft are excluded from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:46.400 --> 00:39:52.240
entering the the air space and they tend to&nbsp;
be used under constant human supervision and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:52.240 --> 00:39:58.320
with the ability for the users to intervene and&nbsp;
to deactivate the system where necessary. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:59.200 --> 00:40:05.680
all of this, we think speaks to also not only&nbsp;
practical limits but also sort of normative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:05.680 --> 00:40:14.480
limits...things that should remain in place even&nbsp;
as technology allows different uses and what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:14.480 --> 00:40:22.320
are concerned about is that certain trends in&nbsp;
military interests and investments that aim at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:23.440 --> 00:40:29.520
that, that aim at using autonomous weapons&nbsp;
increasingly also target persons directly. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:29.520 --> 00:40:36.400
well as a wider range of objects such as vehicles.&nbsp;
As I said, vehicles may also be used by civilians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:36.400 --> 00:40:44.800
depending on on the target profile, right? Use in&nbsp;
increasingly populated areas even in the midst of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:44.800 --> 00:40:52.480
cities over larger spaces longer periods and&nbsp;
at much larger scale. I think Stuart has has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:52.480 --> 00:40:59.840
spoken to this. So, there are these ideas of the&nbsp;
deploying entire swarms. And then, of course, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:59.840 --> 00:41:05.840
much reduced human supervision. And increasingly&nbsp;
relying on artificial intelligence and machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:05.840 --> 00:41:13.600
learning techniques. And considering the speed&nbsp;
at which these developments are taking place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:15.040 --> 00:41:22.320
we really believe it is urgent that states agree&nbsp;
an effective international response to the, to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:22.320 --> 00:41:30.960
ethical, humanitarian, and legal and security&nbsp;
risks that we see on the horizon. Awesome. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:30.960 --> 00:41:35.760
I think that's also a great point you make too&nbsp;
in grounding this conversation is that we're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:42.160
talking about putting limits on all applications&nbsp;
of AI and all types of weaponry. There's many uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:42.160 --> 00:41:48.320
of AI. You know we think of things like missile&nbsp;
defense systems that don't have the same types of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:48.320 --> 00:41:55.840
raise the same set of concerns and considerations&nbsp;
as using AI and anti-personnel systems, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:57.280 --> 00:42:03.200
Can you maybe, let's maybe switch gears here now&nbsp;
into the - all right so we have this problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.200 --> 00:42:08.720
we know it's urgent. What is it that we can do&nbsp;
about it? And what are policy frameworks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:08.720 --> 00:42:14.240
may be helpful for this? And maybe you know I know&nbsp;
there's especially for Max and Stuart's thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:14.240 --> 00:42:20.480
having been in this for a while what may be other&nbsp;
unhelpful suggestions that may be out there? So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:20.480 --> 00:42:25.920
Maya will you take us through how does the ICRC&nbsp;
think about what should the world do? What is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:25.920 --> 00:42:32.880
policy response that you guys would like to see&nbsp;
happen? Sure. Yeah, so actually earlier this year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:33.440 --> 00:42:42.640
in May the ICRC offered a set of more specific&nbsp;
recommendations to all states with a view to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:42.640 --> 00:42:49.200
supporting state's current efforts at establishing&nbsp;
international limits on autonomous weapons. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:49.200 --> 00:42:54.560
with view to ensuring civilian protection in&nbsp;
war compliance with international humanitarian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.560 --> 00:43:03.120
law. And also ethical acceptability. So concretely&nbsp;
the ICRC recommends that states adopt new legally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:03.120 --> 00:43:11.920
binding rules. In particular, certain prohibitions&nbsp;
and restrictions. And so, one type of autonomous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:11.920 --> 00:43:18.480
weapons that we recommend that states prohibit&nbsp;
are what we call unpredictable autonomous weapons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:20.080 --> 00:43:24.720
These are autonomous weapons that are&nbsp;
designed or used in a manner such that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:24.720 --> 00:43:31.520
their effects cannot sufficiently be understood,&nbsp;
predicted, and explained. And this is of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:32.720 --> 00:43:42.080
a concern that for us is particularly acute with&nbsp;
autonomous weapons that continue to learn targets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:42.080 --> 00:43:49.040
during use. Yeah, that would be one example that&nbsp;
for us we find extremely risky and would like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:49.040 --> 00:43:58.640
to see prohibited. Second, in light of ethical&nbsp;
considerations to safeguard humanity and also to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:58.640 --> 00:44:06.560
uphold respect for international humanitarian&nbsp;
law we recommend that autonomous weapons that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:06.560 --> 00:44:13.840
are designed or used to target persons directly&nbsp;
should be prohibited. And as we've already many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:23.040
of us have said this is grounded in a deep ethical&nbsp;
objections to this sort of sort of killing, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:23.040 --> 00:44:29.760
Life, death decisions basically devolved to&nbsp;
machine processes. And, it also grounded in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:29.760 --> 00:44:35.840
concern particularly at acute concerns that&nbsp;
international humanitarian law may be violated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:37.200 --> 00:44:45.120
in the targeting of persons via on the basis of&nbsp;
target profiles, right ?Because the user is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:46.320 --> 00:44:51.040
selecting a particular person and therefore is not&nbsp;
in a position to reasonably assess whether that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:51.040 --> 00:44:58.640
person at that moment may be targeted. Now, also&nbsp;
with the view to the protection of civilians and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:58.640 --> 00:45:05.040
to safeguard humanity, the ICRC also recommends&nbsp;
that the design and use of all non-prohibited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:05.040 --> 00:45:11.600
autonomous weapons should be strictly regulated&nbsp;
through a combination of limits and requirements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:11.600 --> 00:45:18.320
notably limits on the types of targets such as&nbsp;
constraining them to objects that are typical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:18.320 --> 00:45:24.800
military objects. So, typically that are legally&nbsp;
speaking, military objectives by nature. Such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:24.800 --> 00:45:32.240
enemy weapons or enemy tanks. Limits on the&nbsp;
duration, geographical scope, and the scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:32.240 --> 00:45:41.680
of autonomous weapon use and that is aimed in&nbsp;
particular to enable the users to remain aware of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:41.680 --> 00:45:50.720
the circumstances of an attack enough to actually&nbsp;
to for human judgment and control to be sufficient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:50.720 --> 00:45:58.000
and meaningful in every specific attack. As&nbsp;
well as limits on the situations of use such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:58.000 --> 00:46:02.560
constraining autonomous weapons to situations&nbsp;
where civilians and civilian objects are not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:02.560 --> 00:46:09.840
present. And that is, of course, in particular&nbsp;
because of the the risks to civilians if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:09.840 --> 00:46:15.360
are in the vicinity of targets that may trigger&nbsp;
an autonomous weapon strike. And this risk is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:15.360 --> 00:46:21.200
of course, particularly acute where civilians are&nbsp;
present in high density, such as in the midst of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:21.760 --> 00:46:30.080
cities. And then finally, requirements for human&nbsp;
machine interaction. Notably, to ensure effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:30.080 --> 00:46:35.360
human supervision and timely intervention&nbsp;
and deactivation when this becomes necessary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:36.000 --> 00:46:41.840
So, these are the sorts of recommendations&nbsp;
that we that we that we put out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:43.920 --> 00:46:53.120
welcome reactions from states and continue to&nbsp;
work towards to support states in this direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:54.320 --> 00:46:59.520
Excellent. Thank you, Maya. As we close&nbsp;
in these last few minutes of the panel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:00.480 --> 00:47:06.400
Richard from the civil society side of things&nbsp;
how does Maya's policy prescription sound to you?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:07.280 --> 00:47:11.280
Yeah, I mean, I think we're also pushing&nbsp;
for a legal instrument. And I think it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:11.280 --> 00:47:13.920
we think it needs to be one&nbsp;
that contains prohibitions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:14.960 --> 00:47:18.560
It's gonna ban systems that can't be used&nbsp;
with meaningful human control. It's gonna ban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:18.560 --> 00:47:23.440
systems that would target people. And it needs&nbsp;
to have these other rules positive obligations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:23.440 --> 00:47:28.640
to keep control over the use of systems. Because&nbsp;
I think as we've already discussed on this panel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:28.640 --> 00:47:35.600
we have weapons today that work using sensors in&nbsp;
this way. And we need to stop a sort of slippage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:35.600 --> 00:47:42.000
of those systems into an unacceptable future.&nbsp;
So, the combination of prohibitions and positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:42.000 --> 00:47:47.920
obligations to retain meaningful human control and&nbsp;
to prevent this sort of digital dehumanization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:47.920 --> 00:47:53.920
A couple of things just to add it's states, it's&nbsp;
governments who have to create international law.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:53.920 --> 00:47:59.200
So, in terms of what needs to be done, we need&nbsp;
to push governments to speak in favor of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:00.400 --> 00:48:07.520
responses to push themselves for a legal treaty in&nbsp;
response to this to this issue. And to be honest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:07.520 --> 00:48:11.360
over recent years these issues have been under&nbsp;
discussion in the convention on conventional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:11.360 --> 00:48:17.040
weapons. You mentioned it at the beginning,&nbsp;
it's not a forum that frankly is very likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:17.040 --> 00:48:21.760
to lead to an outcome. Even though the majority&nbsp;
of states now internationally who are engaged in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:21.760 --> 00:48:27.360
these discussions, the majority of states I think&nbsp;
support a movement to to begin negotiations. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:28.320 --> 00:48:33.200
I think that before too long there will be&nbsp;
frustration with this pattern of talks and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:33.200 --> 00:48:38.560
there'll be a movement amongst those states who&nbsp;
do want to make progress on this issue to start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:38.560 --> 00:48:44.880
develop a legal instrument more on their own terms&nbsp;
directly working towards this kind of this kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:44.880 --> 00:48:50.640
outcome. And that leads me really to my to my&nbsp;
final point, which is you know an international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:50.640 --> 00:48:54.720
treaty isn't an end in itself. An international&nbsp;
treaty is an instrument that's got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:54.720 --> 00:48:59.680
fulfill certain purposes. It's got to shape the&nbsp;
normative landscape that we're working in. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:59.680 --> 00:49:04.640
really means the sort of social expectations&nbsp;
what does good behavior look like? what does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:04.640 --> 00:49:09.920
bad behavior unacceptable behavior look like?&nbsp;
And we need a legal instrument that sets out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:09.920 --> 00:49:15.840
that landscape in a particular direction that&nbsp;
sets our expectations of governments. And also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:16.400 --> 00:49:22.080
flowing down from that, sets our expectations for&nbsp;
the tech sector, for commercial companies, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:22.080 --> 00:49:27.440
arms manufacturers, sets the expectations of where&nbsp;
they need to impose limits on their own practices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:28.240 --> 00:49:34.960
Because ultimately, I think we all socially have&nbsp;
a stake in drawing these lines. We need to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:34.960 --> 00:49:40.880
a social landscape amongst ourselves. We have to&nbsp;
have belief in norms and in laws and in social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:40.880 --> 00:49:46.400
standards. We have to have a belief that that we&nbsp;
can constrain you know technology in this respect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:46.400 --> 00:49:51.200
So ultimately it's about feeling empowered.&nbsp;
We need people to feel empowered that we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:51.200 --> 00:49:56.080
draw the lines in this space. And to be honest,&nbsp;
I feel pretty confident. We've done it before on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:56.080 --> 00:50:00.640
other weapons issues as a society. We've&nbsp;
found ways to draw lines and they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:01.520 --> 00:50:06.560
saved thousands and thousands of lives, frankly.&nbsp;
So, I think we should have confidence that we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:06.560 --> 00:50:13.360
do the same on this issue. Amazing. Stuart, I know&nbsp;
you need to leave shortly. What are your thoughts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:13.360 --> 00:50:17.200
on this topic? I know you've been dealing with&nbsp;
these diplomatic discussions for quite some time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:18.800 --> 00:50:23.120
Yeah, so I think the first&nbsp;
thing I would like is that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:25.200 --> 00:50:30.800
various nations who are discussing this&nbsp;
actually have a clear understanding of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:30.800 --> 00:50:35.520
it is that they're talking about. So, for&nbsp;
example, at the moment there's a proposal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:36.320 --> 00:50:43.520
to ban what they call fully autonomous weapons&nbsp;
and what that means is weapons that operate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:43.520 --> 00:50:49.760
completely outside the framework of any human&nbsp;
control. And what that means is weapons that wake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:49.760 --> 00:50:55.040
up in the morning and decide by themselves that&nbsp;
they're going to start a war with another country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:55.040 --> 00:51:01.440
right? In other words, what they would ban is&nbsp;
the conscious, self-aware skynet that controls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:01.440 --> 00:51:06.000
all the terminator robots. In other words,&nbsp;
they would ban something that doesn't exist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:06.880 --> 00:51:12.800
probably will never exist, leaving all other&nbsp;
actual weapons that anyone would actually develop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:13.760 --> 00:51:18.960
legal. So, that's the first problem we've&nbsp;
got to get away from this nonsense that we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:18.960 --> 00:51:23.120
worried about machines becoming conscious&nbsp;
and deciding to go around shooting people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:23.120 --> 00:51:28.800
That is a complete fiction. The other&nbsp;
issue is I'm very concerned about any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:29.440 --> 00:51:37.040
treaty that restricts use But not restricting&nbsp;
weapons. I think the the history shows us that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:37.040 --> 00:51:44.160
only practical way to do this is to ban classes of&nbsp;
weapons. We saw this with chemical weapons. Before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:44.160 --> 00:51:51.760
the First World War they were banned. But not as&nbsp;
a class of weapons. What was banned was the use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:52.320 --> 00:51:56.240
of chemical weapons. You were allowed to develop&nbsp;
them. You were allowed to deploy them. But you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:56.240 --> 00:52:01.520
couldn't use them. And, of course, along comes&nbsp;
World War I, everyone has these big stockpiles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:01.520 --> 00:52:08.640
and guess what? They got used. And so, you&nbsp;
don't want to create that slippery slope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:09.920 --> 00:52:14.160
by allowing the development of these weapons&nbsp;
and then saying oh but we're only going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:14.160 --> 00:52:19.520
use them in these very restricted contexts,&nbsp;
right? Because those very restricted contexts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:19.520 --> 00:52:25.120
you know, as soon as real conflict happens,&nbsp;
you know, those rules are going to go out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:25.120 --> 00:52:31.840
the window. You have to have ways of targeting&nbsp;
the manufacturing of weapons on a large scale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:33.040 --> 00:52:42.880
And I don't think we can afford to to let that&nbsp;
go ahead. Because once it goes ahead as Max said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:43.920 --> 00:52:46.240
life as we know it will change forever.

00:52:48.320 --> 00:52:52.480
Thank you, Stuart. And I know that you have to&nbsp;
leave so I'll also give you a moment here to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:52.480 --> 00:52:56.000
say any last words that you'd&nbsp;
like to say on this issue.

00:52:59.440 --> 00:53:05.520
I would like to say, Last&nbsp;
words, Stuart. My last words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:06.960 --> 00:53:13.840
you know I share Richard's optimism that&nbsp;
we have been able to do this in the past.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:14.880 --> 00:53:21.840
The bans that we have put in place on chemical and&nbsp;
biological weapons have been largely successful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:23.520 --> 00:53:30.560
And, in fact, it was the biologists who pointed&nbsp;
out that creating effective biological weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:30.560 --> 00:53:35.840
would reduce the security of every nation rather&nbsp;
than increasing the security of the nations who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:35.840 --> 00:53:42.800
were producing them. And the same goes for the&nbsp;
development of autonomous weapons. So, I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:42.800 --> 00:53:47.840
the bioweapons example is actually a great one&nbsp;
because when we think about the biological weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:47.840 --> 00:53:53.760
convention that's been a very powerful treaty in&nbsp;
terms of its efficacy over the years. And one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:53.760 --> 00:53:58.880
hasn't really have the same type of verification&nbsp;
regime that we associate with something like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:58.880 --> 00:54:04.000
chemical weapons treaty. And what that treaty did&nbsp;
it's a great example of safeguarding a science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:04.000 --> 00:54:10.000
right? In that case, biology and by taking the&nbsp;
weaponization of biology off of the table, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:10.000 --> 00:54:17.200
not only mitigated the risks of bioweapons but it&nbsp;
also allowed biology to really flourish as a field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:17.200 --> 00:54:21.920
and to grow. And for us to think of biotech as&nbsp;
something that's great and positive and cures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:21.920 --> 00:54:27.520
diseases and gives us new medicines. So, one could&nbsp;
imagine here that there could be a similar future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:27.520 --> 00:54:33.360
for artificial intelligence where if we sort of&nbsp;
take the weaponizing of AI and AI deciding to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:33.360 --> 00:54:38.800
kill people off of the table it helps to open the&nbsp;
doors for the science to really flourish and sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:38.800 --> 00:54:44.320
of reach all of its potential. Max I know this&nbsp;
is something you think about a lot in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:44.320 --> 00:54:49.680
the positive potential of AI. Do you agree with&nbsp;
the others in terms of what should be done about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:49.680 --> 00:54:56.400
this issue and how do you think through action&nbsp;
on this topic? In terms of the optimism there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:56.400 --> 00:55:01.120
on what we can learn from biology, I completely&nbsp;
agree with you. In terms of what should be done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:02.800 --> 00:55:08.000
maybe this is my opportunity to maybe annoy the&nbsp;
rest of you on the panel by saying something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:08.000 --> 00:55:17.120
controversial. I am increasingly convinced that&nbsp;
we actually need two treaties, not one. Artificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:17.120 --> 00:55:23.600
intelligence is such a general-purpose technology&nbsp;
that having one treaty for weapons that contain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:23.600 --> 00:55:31.520
AI is becoming as ridiculous as having a&nbsp;
treaty for all weapons that involve motors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:32.240 --> 00:55:38.720
or explosives, you know. And if you instead ask&nbsp;
what sort of division of weapon types makes sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:39.840 --> 00:55:46.800
it's really very clear that on one hand there are&nbsp;
the big and heavy expensive weapons that are used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:46.800 --> 00:55:53.280
against tanks, airplanes, ships, and so on. They&nbsp;
need to be big because you need a lot of power to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:53.280 --> 00:55:58.640
blow through a tank or shoot down an airplane,&nbsp;
and on the other hand you have the weapons that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:58.640 --> 00:56:04.880
the ICRC wants to ban, which target humans&nbsp;
specifically. The reason they can be so tiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:05.760 --> 00:56:11.840
and small and cheap is because we humans are not&nbsp;
heavily armored, right, and those are precisely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:11.840 --> 00:56:18.240
the weapons that because of their low price&nbsp;
and low mass you can become weapons of mass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:18.240 --> 00:56:24.720
destruction where where very few can kill very&nbsp;
many. I think we should have completely separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:24.720 --> 00:56:29.840
treaties for the two of them. I think for&nbsp;
the first kind the big expensive ones the CCW&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:32.080 --> 00:56:37.600
might be a reasonable forum for this because&nbsp;
of course it was as was mentioned already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:39.200 --> 00:56:43.200
unpredictable weapons that are&nbsp;
intrinsically destabilizing and maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:43.200 --> 00:56:47.760
will cause the US and China to accidentally&nbsp;
get into a nuclear war with each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:47.760 --> 00:56:51.040
That's not in China's interest&nbsp;
or in the US's interest and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:52.800 --> 00:57:01.120
that can give the superpowers an incentive to&nbsp;
go to CCW and agree on various norms and rules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:02.960 --> 00:57:08.880
for the military aspects here. For the big heavy&nbsp;
expensive weapons. The other stuff doesn't belong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:08.880 --> 00:57:15.600
in the CCW at all, right? The CCW is about a&nbsp;
forum for discussing military weapons. It has the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:15.600 --> 00:57:20.480
word "conventional" in the name there's nothing&nbsp;
conventional about weapons of mass destruction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:20.480 --> 00:57:28.000
where one dude can kill 100,000 people by pressing&nbsp;
a button and unleashing a slaughterbot swarm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:28.000 --> 00:57:32.800
And as we already discussed right?, the main&nbsp;
victims here, the main use cases are civilian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:33.680 --> 00:57:41.120
not military. So, I would like to see in parallel&nbsp;
with the CCW a new forum focused specifically on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:41.120 --> 00:57:46.880
weapons that target humans from not a military&nbsp;
perspective but from a humanitarian perspective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:46.880 --> 00:57:52.560
saying this is a disaster for civilization as&nbsp;
we know it. It'll cause the mother of all tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:52.560 --> 00:57:58.880
clashes against the AI field and also thereby rob&nbsp;
us of all the great benefits we could get from it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:08.080
and let these two processes play out in parallel&nbsp;
not wait for years and years and years while the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:08.080 --> 00:58:14.000
CCW discusses military things to have all&nbsp;
these civilian things get out of control in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:14.000 --> 00:58:22.480
the meantime. Let's see if I annoyed anyone but I&nbsp;
really think this makes sense. Annoyance is good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:23.520 --> 00:58:28.560
but I think while we've heard a lot of different&nbsp;
perspectives here on the "what we should do about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:28.560 --> 00:58:35.440
it" there is some fundamentals that go across all&nbsp;
of the thinking which is this idea of needing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:35.440 --> 00:58:41.280
draw a line and needing to prohibit certain types&nbsp;
of weapons and needing to do that in a forum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:41.280 --> 00:58:46.560
factor that is a legally binding treaty. And it's&nbsp;
not some sort of political promise or anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:46.560 --> 00:58:52.640
sort of more measly than that. Something that&nbsp;
really is new law around these threats that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:52.640 --> 00:58:59.280
face. I really want to just take this moment as we&nbsp;
come to a close here to thank everyone. It's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:59.280 --> 00:59:05.520
so awesome talking with all of you and we'll just&nbsp;
do one more round of passing the mic here. I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:05.520 --> 00:59:11.440
all of us choose and care deeply about this&nbsp;
issue that we dedicate years of our time to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:12.320 --> 00:59:18.480
and thinking through this so you know any last&nbsp;
thoughts that you have or perspectives that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:18.480 --> 00:59:26.800
want to share. Maya I'll start with you. Thanks,&nbsp;
EmIlia. Yeah perhaps just to come back to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:26.800 --> 00:59:32.960
moment of opportunity that we have here. There's&nbsp;
a real momentum in the discussions on autonomous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:32.960 --> 00:59:39.280
weapons systems that are underway. The positions&nbsp;
of states are becoming increasingly nuanced and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:39.280 --> 00:59:45.920
sophisticated and with that they also discover&nbsp;
areas of agreement where before they weren't aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:45.920 --> 00:59:55.600
of that. I also think just reflecting a little bit&nbsp;
on what Max just said, this issue like many others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:55.600 --> 01:00:01.920
that will be on the horizon I think in the in the&nbsp;
future has the potential for enormous complexity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:01.920 --> 01:00:11.120
But for political action is sometimes necessary&nbsp;
to focus on some fundamentals, elementary things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:11.680 --> 01:00:20.800
So rather than getting too too distracted by the&nbsp;
very the various forms that autonomous weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:20.800 --> 01:00:26.880
can take, we would recommend taking sort&nbsp;
of a what we like to call a more technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:26.880 --> 01:00:36.080
agnostic approach. An approach that focuses&nbsp;
on the human within this use of force process&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:36.080 --> 01:00:44.080
and really aims at preserving and maintaining&nbsp;
sufficient human judgment and control. And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:44.800 --> 01:00:51.760
norms around this we can articulate them and we&nbsp;
can formalize them in the form of internationally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:51.760 --> 01:01:00.080
legally binding norms without having all the&nbsp;
specificities, all the information about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:00.080 --> 01:01:06.080
autonomous weapons will look like in the future,&nbsp;
what they can and cannot do, and so on. And so for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:08.080 --> 01:01:16.400
more generally I would say that discussions in the&nbsp;
CCW and wider discussions on various applications&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:16.400 --> 01:01:21.920
of developments in science and technology I&nbsp;
think they really benefit from diverse people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:21.920 --> 01:01:30.160
contributing to them. And contributing&nbsp;
in particular to debates deliberations&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:30.160 --> 01:01:38.080
on the acceptability and the desirability of new&nbsp;
weapons and of course technologists, philosophers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:38.080 --> 01:01:44.640
social scientists, and other knowledgeable people&nbsp;
really all have a stake in this because ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:44.640 --> 01:01:52.880
this is about our common humanity about the rules&nbsp;
that protect us all in in case in case of war. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:52.880 --> 01:01:58.400
so it's really critical to foster these sorts&nbsp;
of dialogues among different communities of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:58.400 --> 01:02:03.600
practice and the wider public and the Future&nbsp;
of Life Institute is doing a great job at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:04.400 --> 01:02:09.120
supporting at enabling these sorts of&nbsp;
discussions. So, thank you very much for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:10.080 --> 01:02:15.520
Thank you Maya. So well said and we so deeply&nbsp;
respect the work that you do and the ICRC does&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:15.520 --> 01:02:21.840
and I'm so thrilled to have you with us today.&nbsp;
So we we greatly appreciate it as well. Richard&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:21.840 --> 01:02:26.400
you're kind of closing thoughts? Yeah, thanks&nbsp;
Emilia. And perhaps picking up on some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

